Slide 2 – Project Overview
Objective

This project builds an end-to-end Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline for research paper analysis.

The system processes a research paper, cleans textual content, generates multiple chunking strategies, creates semantic embeddings, retrieves the most relevant information, evaluates retrieval quality, and stores the embeddings inside a vector database for efficient semantic search.

Key Goals
Extract text from research papers
Clean noisy PDF content
Compare chunking strategies
Generate embeddings
Retrieve relevant context
Evaluate retrieval accuracy
Store embeddings inside ChromaDB
Slide 3 – Problem Statement

Traditional keyword search struggles to retrieve relevant information from lengthy research papers because it depends heavily on exact word matching.

Challenges include:

Large document size
Context fragmentation
Poor semantic understanding
Duplicate information
Low retrieval precision

The proposed RAG pipeline addresses these issues through semantic chunking, dense vector embeddings, and vector similarity search.

